Despite the convenience of rapidity, the particle mass (particle species) is not easy to measure in experiments. The pseudo-rapidity \(\eta\) is often used as a substitute for \(y\). The \(\eta\) is given by the particle momentum polar angle \(\theta\) relative to the beam axis:

\[\eta=-{\rm ln}\tan\frac{\theta}{2}=\frac{1}{2}{\rm ln}\frac{|\vec{p}|+p_{z}}{ |\vec{p}|-p_{z}}.\] (A.7)

For massless particles, \(\eta=y\). For mid-rapidity particles, \(\beta_{z}\ll 1\): \(\eta\approx y\). Similarly the following relations are useful:

